React compares props by reference, not by value. `{ color: "red" }` creates a new object each time the parent renders, so the child sees a different prop even when the contents match.

But the object alone doesn't cause the render. A child re-renders whenever its parent re-renders, memoized or not. The object only matters when something skips work based on prop identity, like `React.memo`, or a `useEffect`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` dependency, or a context value.

```jsx
function Parent({ count }) {
  const style = { color: "red", size: 12 };
  return <Child style={style} count={count} />;
}

const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style, count }) {
  /* ... */
});
```

When `count` changes, `style` is a new object, so `memo` can't skip Child. If `count` stays the same but Parent re-renders for some other reason, Child still renders because `style` is new.

Smallest fixes, roughly in order:

1. If the object is constant, move it outside the component. No hooks, no cost.
2. If it depends on props or state, wrap it: `const style = useMemo(() => ({ color, size }), [color, size]);`
3. Pass primitives instead: `<Child color={color} size={size} />`. `memo` compares strings and numbers by value, so this works without any hooks.
4. Custom comparison as the second argument to `React.memo`. Usually not worth the maintenance.

Before changing anything, confirm which prop is changing. The React DevTools profiler has a "why did this render" option, or you can log the props inside the child. Also, if the child isn't wrapped in `memo` and no effect depends on the object, memoizing it won't change anything. If you're using the React Compiler, it may auto-memoize some of this, but check your build setup since I may be out of date on that.

